      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "First, we need to determine the total number of ways in which the four team members can run the first three laps. This can be calculated by considering the total number of permutations of the four individuals there are to run, and then dividing it by the number of ways that Jordan can run the fourth lap - which is 1 way. To see how many total ways there are to run the first three laps, we have to choose someone other than Jordan for the first, second, and third laps, and there are four persons, fourteen possible combinations. Of those, only one combination has Jordan finishing last. So there must be **14/3! = 7** ways this can happen for the first three laps. Of those, there are seven ways for Jordan to run the last lap, as is required if we have considered the other three also having to run the first three laps. Therefore, the total number of different ways for the four team members to run the race, where Jordan is fixed in the last position, is **7 ways** x **1 way** for Jordan running first = **7** total combinations.",
