    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We can think of the team members as being assigned to the four laps in order: J, J, _, _, _, (n is some random configuration for each lap).\n\nWe know that J runs the fourth lap. So we don't need to worry about that.  $\\text{First Lap: non-J} \\cdot \\text{Second Lap: non-J} \\cdot \\text{Third Lap: non-J} \\cdot \\text{Fourth Lap: J}$  $\\Longrightarrow$  The group of 3 non-Js can be assigned to the three laps in $3!$ (or $2 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1$) ways.  This is our first step.\n\nThe team now \"settles into place\" and we have the following arrangement: J, _, _, _, _.\n\nThere are $\\text{Four non-Js}$ to be assigned to $4$ other positions. That can be done in $4!$ ways.  This is our second step.\n\nOur final answer is $3! \\cdot 4! = 24 \\cdot 24 = 576$ cases.\nThe answer is 576",
