    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of different orders in which the four team members can run, assuming that Jordan (the fastest runner) runs the last lap.\n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Fixing Jordan on the Fourth Lap:**\n   - Since Jordan will run the fourth lap, we fix him at the fourth position.\n   - Now, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange the remaining three runners on the first three positions.\n\n2. **Arranging the Remaining Three Runners:**\n   - The number of ways to arrange three runners in three positions is given by the factorial of 3, denoted as \\(3!\\).\n   - The factorial of a number \\(n\\) (denoted \\(n!\\)) is the product of all positive integers up to \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\n3. **Interpreting the Factorial:**\n   - \\(3!\\) represents the number of permutations (arrangements) of the three runners.\n   - For example, different arrangements would be: \\(A, B, C\\), \\(A, C, B\\), \\(B, A, C\\), \\(B, C, A\\), \\(C, A, B\\), \\(C, B, A\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - Therefore, there are 6 different orders in which the four team members (including Jordan) can run the relay race.\n\nThus, the total number of different orders in which the four team members can run, given that Jordan runs the fourth lap, is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
